From c7e3348def349446159e001134ab246260762939 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Snitzer <snitzer@kernel.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 04:34:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] SUNRPC: Add svc_thread_chosen_to_stop()

A service thread's loop calls svc_thread_should_stop() once a request
before it calls svc_recv() again, and svc_recv() calls it again after
its wait.  Each call reads the pool's sp_flags, which other CPUs write
while the thread handles its request, so the loop's call takes a cache
miss on a busy pool for a check svc_recv() is about to repeat.

Add svc_thread_chosen_to_stop(), which reads only RQ_VICTIM in the
thread's own rq_flags: svc_recv() sets it in the thread that takes
SP_NEED_VICTIM, on every path that returns without a request.

Signed-off-by: Mike Snitzer <snitzer@kernel.org>
---
 include/linux/sunrpc/svc.h | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/linux/sunrpc/svc.h b/include/linux/sunrpc/svc.h
index 311d14aa6355..3bb4a084b21b 100644
--- a/include/linux/sunrpc/svc.h
+++ b/include/linux/sunrpc/svc.h
@@ -560,6 +560,24 @@ static inline bool svc_thread_should_stop(struct svc_rqst *rqstp)
 	return test_bit(RQ_VICTIM, &rqstp->rq_flags);
 }
 
+/**
+ * svc_thread_chosen_to_stop - check if this thread has been told to stop
+ * @rqstp: the thread that might need to stop
+ *
+ * svc_recv() calls svc_thread_should_stop() on each pass, which makes the
+ * thread that takes SP_NEED_VICTIM a victim with RQ_VICTIM.  A thread's
+ * loop around svc_recv() can read that flag in its own rq_flags instead
+ * of the pool's flags, which other CPUs write while it handles a request.
+ *
+ * Return values:
+ *   %true: caller should invoke svc_exit_thread()
+ *   %false: caller should do nothing
+ */
+static inline bool svc_thread_chosen_to_stop(const struct svc_rqst *rqstp)
+{
+	return test_bit(RQ_VICTIM, &rqstp->rq_flags);
+}
+
 /**
  * svc_thread_init_status - report whether thread has initialised successfully
  * @rqstp: the thread in question
-- 
2.52.0

